  "stdout": "\n### 6.2 Alignment Research\n\nAI alignment research focuses on ensuring that AI systems pursue objectives that are consistent with human values and intentions. This includes research on reward modeling, constitutional AI, and other techniques for aligning system behavior with human preferences.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nTrustworthy Machine Learning represents a critical frontier in AI research and deployment. This paper has reviewed recent advances across multiple dimensions of trustworthiness, including model robustness, fairness, privacy, and safety. Key contributions to the field include foundational architectural innovations such as deep residual learning [1], comprehensive trustworthiness assessment frameworks like DecodingTrust [2], and automated red-teaming methodologies like RedCodeAgent [3].\n\nDespite significant progress, several challenges remain. First, there is a need for more comprehensive and standardized evaluation frameworks that can assess trustworthiness across diverse applications. Second, the trade-offs between different trustworthiness dimensions (e.g., between performance and robustness, or between privacy and utility) require further investigation. Third, as AI systems become more capable and autonomous, new trustworthiness challenges will emerge that demand novel research approaches.\n\nFuture work should focus on developing unified frameworks that integrate multiple trustworthiness dimensions, creating more efficient and scalable evaluation methodologies, and establishing best practices for trustworthy ML deployment in real-world applications. The continued advancement of trustworthy ML is essential for realizing the full potential of AI while mitigating risks and building public trust.\n\n## References\n\n[1] K. He, X. Zhang, S. Ren, and J. Sun, \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition,\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n\n[2] B. Wang, W. Chen, H. Pei, et al., \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models,\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[3] C. Guo, C. Xie, Y. Yang, et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[4] C. Dwork and A. Roth, \"The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy,\" Foundations and Trends in Theoretical Computer Science, 2014.\n\n[5] B. McMahan, E. Moore, D. Ramage, et al., \"Communication-Efficient Learning of Deep Networks from Decentralized Data,\" AISTATS, 2017.\n\n[6] I. Goodfellow, J. Shlens, and C. Szegedy, \"Explaining and Harnessing Adversarial Examples,\" ICLR, 2015.\n\n[7] R. Guidotti, A. Monreale, S. Ruggieri, et al., \"A Survey of Methods for Explaining Black Box Models,\" ACM Computing Surveys, 2019.\n\n[8] M. D. Zeiler and R. Fergus, \"Visualizing and Understanding Convolutional Networks,\" ECCV, 2014.\n",
